;;; Commentary:

;; Noir is Ubiquitty's house theme: a soft off-white on a deep near-black,
;; six accents at controlled chroma so each reads as its hue, and bright
;; twins that are lighter, never grey except bright-black.  This file is the
;; same palette as the "Noir" table of Source/Core/Themes/builtin-themes.toml
;; applied to Emacs faces, so an Emacs frame and a Ubiquitty pane on the
;; same screen share one set of colours.
;;
;; The palette has sixteen ANSI colours, a foreground, a background and a
;; cursor.  An editor also needs surfaces that a terminal never draws: the
;; mode line, the current line, the region, diff backgrounds.  Those are
;; derived the way the app derives its tab-strip band (TabBand.h), in Oklch
;; on the background's own hue: lightness lifted by a fixed step, chroma held
;; at max(0.012, 0.95 * C) so the near-black stays on its cool axis rather
;; than greying out.  The band at L+0.06 is #23252b, the value the app paints
;; its tab strip with; the mode line uses it for the same reason.  Tinted
;; surfaces (region, search, diff) take an accent's hue at that same low
;; lightness so they read as "the accent, dimmed" instead of a new colour.
;;
;; Every colour is written as "#rrggbb".  A truecolor tty (COLORTERM set to
;; truecolor, which the Ubiquitty host exports) shows them exactly; a
;; 256-colour tty gets Emacs's nearest approximation.
;;
;; Load with `M-x load-theme RET ubiquitty-noir', or from init.el:
;;
;;   (add-to-list 'custom-theme-load-path "/path/to/Source/Platform/Emacs")
;;   (load-theme 'ubiquitty-noir t)

;;; Code:

(deftheme ubiquitty-noir
  "Ubiquitty's Noir: a soft off-white on a deep near-black, accents at controlled chroma."
  :background-mode 'dark
  :kind 'color-scheme
  :family 'ubiquitty)

